== Abstract
We replicate three recent model-compression papers — MKA (*Pruning via Merging*:
diffusion-map based *layer merging*, Liu et al. 2024), CoM (*Chain-of-Merging*:
geodesic *SLERP* layer fusion, Zhang et al. 2026), and SparC3 (*ε-LRP
attribution-guided pruning*, Hatefi, Dreyer et al. 2025) — on a from-scratch
MNIST MLP (≈0.47M params, no pretrained checkpoints, no closed-source models). The
project's compression side reports _real reductions_: greedy layer merging of the
residual MLP collapses 4→1 hidden layers, cutting parameters by _42.3\%_ and FLOPs by
_42.2\%_ while retaining _99.29\% ± 0.02\%_ of test accuracy across three
initializations; LRP-pruning at 50\% sparsity retains _73.8\% ± 6.8\%_ (LRP beats random
at every sparsity, magnitude stays ahead on clean MNIST — an honest nuance). The central
pattern we find is that *high inter-layer redundancy (diffusion-map NMI) is necessary but
_not_ sufficient for lossless fusion*: the plain MLP has even higher NMI yet collapses to
random chance after a single merge, whereas the residual architecture's skip connections
are what make α=$S_(l m)$ fusion near-lossless. Every number here is generated by this repo
(`pytest`: 78 green tests; notebooks execute per phase).

== 1. Introduction
Neural networks are massively over-parameterized. Two families of techniques exploit that
redundancy with opposite philosophies: *layer merging* fuses similar activations into
fewer, wider weights (#underline[next]) (removing entire transformation stages), while
*pruning* zeroes low-importance weights (#underline[inside]) (leaving the graph,
shrinking the storage). This project reproduces both ends on one controlled task and,
crucially, quantifies the *reduction side* of merging — parameters, FLOPs, model size,
latency — not just the accuracy after compression.

#fig("similarity_all.png", "Phase 2 — layer-redundancy maps. Left: MKA's diffusion-map/NMI; right: cosine. Top: residual, bottom: plain. The residual graph shows exploitable redundancy (off-diag NMI mean 0.512, max 0.988); the plain graph's *high* NMI (mean 0.525, max 1.000) is our central cautionary finding.")

== 2. Related Work
We implement the three assigned papers as a coherent, mutually-complementary pipeline:

+ *MKA — Pruning via Merging* (Liu et al. 2024, EMNLP): a diffusion map
  embeds layer activations; pair-wise redundancy is scored with a joint-Gaussian NMI; the
  most similar pair is fused with weight factor α = $S_(l m)$.
+ *CoM — Beyond Layer-Wise Merging* (Zhang et al. 2026, CVPR): weight
  tensors are merged along their geodesic (SLERP), with *non-positional* pairing of the
  most similar tensors — the α=$S_(l m)$ weighted strategy is CoM's per-tensor specialization.
+ *SparC3 — Attribution-Guided Pruning* (Hatefi, Dreyer et al. 2025): every weight receives an explanation
  (ε-LRP relevance), then the least-relevant global fraction q is zeroed.

A broader survey — pruning, low-rank factorization, quantization, distillation,
weight-sharing, and cross-model merging — is documented in `docs/other_approaches.md`;
our own derivations and formulas live in `docs/approach.md`.

== 3. Datasets and Models
*Dataset.* MNIST (60k train / 10k test, 28×28). Loaded from raw IDX via our own
`src/data/mnist.py` (no torchvision dependency), normalized to `[0,1]`.

*Models.* #raw("SimpleMLP(input_dim, hidden_dims, num_classes, dropout)") :
784-256-256-256-256-10 (400,906 params). Its residual sibling
#raw("ResidualMLP") adds a scaling-branch residual connection
(`branch_scale = 0.1`) and a linear projection for the first fusion — the skip
connection is exactly what makes layer merging safe (finding F1). Config is a single
dataclass (`src/config/default.py`), overridable via `.env`.

== 4. Experimental Plan
Eight reproducible phases (one script + one notebook each), every phase writing
machine-readable JSON/CSV to `artifacts/`:

+ Baseline — train/load; footprint snapshot (accuracy, params, FLOPs, size, latency).
+ Similarity — diffusion map embeddings; NMI vs cosine redundancy maps (Fig. 1).
+ Merging — greedy α=$S_(l m)$ fusion; per-merge evidence: which pair, what α, what cost.
+ Strategies — same pair fused via weighted/uniform/SLERP; α recorded for each.
+ α-ablation — the same pair at every α ∈ {0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9}.
+ Pruning — LRP vs magnitude vs random across a sparsity sweep; per-sparsity accuracy.
+ Pipeline — both tracks end-to-end; full before/after evidence table + Gantt.
+ Feasibility — honest no-go evidence: merge-on-plain collapses, LRP-on-residual is undefined.

Reproduction: `scripts/reproduce.py` retrains seeds {42, 7, 2026} and replays both
tracks, reporting mean ± std. `make run-all` = tests + phases + notebooks + reproduce.
A study path restating every formula below with the repo's actual numbers lives in
`docs/learning/` (`00_roadmap.md` → per-paper calculations `02/…/04` → code guide `06`).

== 5. Methodology (summary)
*Diffusion map.* On activations of layer $l$, build $K_(i j) = exp(-||h_i - h_j||^2 / sigma)$, normalize to a row-stochastic $P = D^(-1) K$, take eigen-multipliers (largest non-trivial eigenvectors) as coordinates.

*Similarity (MKA).* For layers $l, m$, fit a joint Gaussian on the embedded coordinates
and compute mutual information $I = 1/2 ln( |Sigma_l| dot |Sigma_m| / |Sigma_(l m)| )$
(general form; reduces to $-1/2 ln(1 - rho^2)$ for two 1-D Gaussians with correlation
$rho$). The standardized NMI is the score. We also report cosine similarity on the
same embeddings as a baseline.

*Merge rule.* When layers $l, m$ are fused we replace $(W_l, b_l, W_m, b_m)$ by one pair
using factor $alpha$: weighted $W_("merged") = alpha W_l + (1 - alpha) W_m)$ with
$alpha = S_(l m)$ (MKA); uniform fixes $alpha = 0.5$; SLERP interpolates along the matrix
geodesic $W_(t) = (sin((1-t) theta) W_l + sin(t theta) W_m) / sin(theta)$ where $theta$ is the angle between $W_l, W_m$ (CoM).
Pairs are chosen *non-positionally* by similarity, not adjacency.

*LRP pruning (SparC3).* Relevance $R_j = sum_k (a_j W_(j k)) / (sum_(j') a_(j') W_(j' k) + epsilon) R_k$ is
propagated from the logit to every weight; the global bottom-$q$ fraction of
weights is zeroed.

== 6. Results
=== 6.1 Baseline
A 6-epoch residual MLP reaches _0.9775_ test accuracy (466,698 params, 930,822 FLOPs,
1.87 MB FP32). Plain MLP: 0.9760 (400,906 params).

