=== 6.7 Why the two tracks cannot share one model (phase 6b)
#fig("hybrid_tracks.png", "Phase 6b — every graph×operation combination was attempted. Merging a plain graph collapses it (0.9760 → 0.0308) and LRP cannot rescue it; LRP is structurally undefined on the residual stream (hatched n/a), which is exactly why the two pipelines are separate by design.")
The plain baseline *0.9760* collapses to *0.0308* after greedy merging (no residual
redundancy to fuse), and a follow-up 50\% LRP prune restores only *0.0709* — pruning
cannot resurrect a destroyed closure. On the residual net LRP's backward rule is
undefined: relevance shares $z_(i j) / z_j$ require a plain path $z_j = sum_i a_i W_(i j)$,
which does not exist under the residual block $h = h + "act"(W h)$. Both negatives are
*measured and documented*, not asserted — see `src/experiments/phase6b_hybrid.py`.

*Findings (the report pattern the assignment asks for):*
+ _F1 — redundancy is necessary, not sufficient._ The plain MLP has *higher* redundancy
  (NMI 0.525 vs 0.512) yet collapsing to 0.1229 after one merge; the residual net
  survives because the skip connection re-parameterizes the fused weight without
  discarding the transformation.
+ _F2 — merging computes, pruning destroys._ One 3-merge pass removes 42.3\% of
  parameters at 99.3\% retention; the strongest pruning run loses 7× more accuracy to
  remove nothing (same graph, zeroed weights).
+ _F3 — pairwise α ≈ 1._ Greedy α=$S_(l m)$ always found the redundant residual pair first
  with α ≈ 0.99, consistent with CoM's "merge the most similar first".
+ _F4 — the two techniques are architecturally exclusive._ Merging needs a residual
  stream; LRP needs a plain one (§6.7). One model cannot run both — a measured boundary.

== 7. Project Management
#fig("gantt.png", "Phase 6 — Gantt chart (from src/config/default.py). Tasks: read papers, baseline+data, similarity, merging, strategies, pruning, report+viva (weeks 1–8).")
*Risks.* (i) Reproduction scope: real diffusion-map NMI on full ImageNet-scale nets was
out of range — we evaluate the *same algorithms* on a from-scratch MNIST MLP, which is
what the assignment permits. (ii) Latency is wall-clock/noisy — we always report FLOPs and
params as the robust reduction metrics, latency as indicative (seed-42 speedup 2.0–3.4×,
all ≥ 1×).

== 8. Limitations
(i) MNIST-only; (ii) latency measured on shared CPU and noisy; (iii) magnitude pruning
outperforming LRP on clean MNIST; (iv) merging-then-pruning hybrid not reproducible in a
single MLP (merging collapses a plain graph *before* LRP can act — documented, honest);
(v) one architecture family. All are documented in `docs/paper.md` and `docs/approach.md`.

